  <div class="post-meta"><span>2026</span><span class="blog-tag">IRS News</span><span>Guzman Tax Accounting</span></div>
  <div class="post-featured-img"><img src="/assets/taxes-para-llc-en-espanol.webp" alt="LLC Taxes in Spanish" loading="lazy"/></div>
  <div class="post-content"><p>Having an LLC in the United States is one of the best decisions a Latino entrepreneur can make. But when tax season arrives, many LLC owners don't know exactly what they need to file, when, or how. And that lack of knowledge can be costly.</p><h2>What is an LLC and how does the IRS treat it?</h2><p>An LLC, or Limited Liability Company, is a legal structure that protects your personal assets from business debts. The IRS can treat it differently depending on how it's set up:</p><ul><li>Single-member LLC — By default, the IRS treats it as a sole proprietorship. You report business income on your personal return using Schedule C.</li><li>Multi-member LLC — The IRS treats it as a partnership by default. It requires a separate return using Form 1065, and each member receives a Schedule K-1.</li><li>LLC with S-Corp election — If your LLC elected to be treated as an S-Corp, you file using Form 1120-S. This can significantly reduce your taxes if the business generates strong income.</li></ul><h2>When do you need to file taxes if you have an LLC?</h2><p>It depends on your structure, but in general:</p><ul><li>Sole proprietor / single-member LLC — April 15 each year</li><li>Partnership / multi-member LLC — March 15 each year</li><li>S-Corp — March 15 each year</li></ul><p>Filing late triggers automatic IRS penalties even if you don't owe money.</p><h2>What deductions apply to an LLC?</h2><p>An LLC can legally deduct many business expenses to reduce what it pays the IRS. The most common deductions include office rent, equipment, a vehicle used for business, advertising, software, professional services like accounting and legal, and business insurance.</p><p>Many LLC owners lose hundreds or thousands of dollars every year by not knowing all the deductions available to them.</p><h2>What happens if you don't file your LLC's taxes?</h2><p>The IRS applies automatic penalties for late or incorrect filings. If your LLC has multiple members, penalties can run into hundreds of dollars per month for each member. Ignoring the obligation doesn't make it go away — the debt grows with interest until it's resolved.</p><h2>Do I need an accountant to file my LLC's taxes?</h2><p>Technically you can do it yourself, but mistakes on LLC returns are very common and very costly. An accountant who specializes in Spanish-language LLC taxes knows exactly which forms to use, which deductions to apply, and how to legally reduce what you pay the IRS.</p><h2>At Guzman Tax Accounting, we handle your LLC's taxes</h2><p>At Guzman Tax Accounting, we specialize in LLC taxes in Spanish in Las Vegas. We explain the entire process clearly, prepare your return correctly, and make sure you take advantage of every deduction you're entitled to. Call us at (725) 340-6758 or reach out on WhatsApp.</p><a href="/en/consultation" class="btn btn-primary" style="margin-top:8px;">Get a Consultation</a></div>
